nant ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/706f896b329f2d7da4eb32dd48f7b3f81ab6b7d7b1dbc40181dcb9651fb94ff5.png) we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/dd7fd8d8eb625e1661dee2d7d418cf4be1c23f20a90932c4106d4b9b940709c4.png)\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/e505fe33cc7f6db13c021beaddcf71823fe506bc914c45c8c24520d905b1c8d7.png) is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/557ab1b22319727accc0f327cc1981a28de2f44f4e6c4cba8c55fc9863b3cc81.png) has a local minimum at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/6f22d6fba010e28fb4e39bbeb1d228d2d5fce35b324d55bdf0c209a983f7d970.png) and a saddle point at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/6aed8e1c686dfe44296c0f23dd69bb4442ad30864f5600c63b0ceff16da7b576.png)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186124136 1145 "The local extrema of $f$ occurs at its critical points. A point $\\mathbf{a}$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\n(for stationary points) or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{a})$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a)$, we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0$, then:if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$ if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0$, then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} - 3 = 0; 2y = 0\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 1\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=0\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) = 6x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y) = 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) = 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0)$, we get\n$H(1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)) = 6 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = 12 > 0$.\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,0))_{11}= 6>0$, the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$.\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(-1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,0)$, we get\n$H(-1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(-1,0)) = -6\\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -12 < 0$\nTherefore, $f(-1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,0)$." 13194139535812 false] [17592186124574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367662 13194139535812 true] [17592186124574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367669 13194139535812 true] [17592186124574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367718 13194139535812 true] [17592186124574 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367725 13194139535812 true] [17592186124575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367676 13194139535812 true] [17592186124575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367683 13194139535812 true] [17592186124575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367711 13194139535812 true] [17592186124575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367739 13194139535812 true] [17592186124576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367690 13194139535812 true] [17592186124576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367697 13194139535812 true] [17592186124576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367704 13194139535812 true] [17592186124576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367732 13194139535812 true] [17592186124576 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367746 13194139535812 true] [17592186124920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535812 true] [17592186124920 1100 "Determine the stationary points of $f(x, y) = 2\\ln x - 2xy + y^{2}$." 13194139535812 false] [17592186124920 1100 "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2xy+{y}^{2}.$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186124920 1145 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-2y=0 \\\\ -2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{(-1)}=-1 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$\nWe reject the point $(-1,-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(1,1).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186124997 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535812 true] [17592186124997 1100 "The function\n$f(x, y)$\nhas the first derivative\n$f^{′}(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y\\end{bmatrix}$,\nand has a stationary point at $(0, 0)$. If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $H(0, 0) > 0$\n2. $\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}}(0, 0) < 0$\n3. The point $(0, 0)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139535812 false] [17592186124997 1100 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186124997 1145 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367663 13194139535812 true] [17592186367662 1089 "q-102328" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535812 true] [17592186367662 1100 "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x-2y+12.$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367662 1113 #uuid "493c2503-d927-5c33-a8cb-65c2e910cf8f" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367662 1145 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6 & 2y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6=0 \\\\ 2y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,1).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367664 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367665 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367666 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367667 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367668 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367667 13194139535812 true] [17592186367663 1110 #uuid "9b6e59c8-6bfb-535c-9de6-19e6f89eaff3" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367664 1034 "(-1,3)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367664 1111 #uuid "4107d8ff-393c-588d-855e-f98929daa1df" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367665 1034 "(1,3)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367665 1111 #uuid "e7cab519-a103-5625-874a-77b49134560e" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367666 1034 "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367666 1111 #uuid "75c146fc-c19c-5dbd-952f-67cd4eb2e410" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367667 1034 "(3,1)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367667 1111 #uuid "45aabc16-1f22-5c5c-8b94-974d01985d24" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367668 1034 "$(1,1)$ and $(3,1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367668 1111 #uuid "ce08e2c3-f8c8-529e-a272-e965219ef45a" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367669 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367670 13194139535812 true] [17592186367669 1089 "q-103925" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367669 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535812 true] [17592186367669 1100 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+xy-3x+3y.$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367669 1113 #uuid "ba793541-bf07-5369-9d6f-63098b7e3fb4" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367669 1145 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y+x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3 & 2y+x+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3=0 \\\\ 2y+x+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=3-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3-2x)+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 6-4x+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 9-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $y=3-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3-2(3)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,-3).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,-3).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367671 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367672 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367673 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367674 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367675 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367675 13194139535812 true] [17592186367670 1110 #uuid "6aee779d-08e4-572f-bf5c-a219f164a7d8" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367671 1034 "(-3,6)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367671 1111 #uuid "e3b73635-070a-5893-bb2d-84a8f01d3b63" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367672 1034 "(3,-2)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367672 1111 #uuid "21960eef-4077-505e-875f-2df261e215b8" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367673 1034 "(6,-9)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367673 1111 #uuid "e44238b9-bf24-5223-b153-88832c918613" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367674 1034 "(-3,9)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367674 1111 #uuid "429cf398-eb87-536b-9bdb-354ba539802e" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367675 1034 "(3,-3)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367675 1111 #uuid "dca5c2ea-c4f7-5266-b3cc-831ac8ea2527" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367676 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367677 13194139535812 true] [17592186367676 1089 "q-104984" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367676 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535812 true] [17592186367676 1100 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367676 1113 #uuid "8e4002c3-ae8f-50f5-b36b-347ebe1b0f51" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367676 1145 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367678 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367679 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367680 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367681 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367682 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367680 13194139535812 true] [17592186367677 1110 #uuid "ea924284-0cc4-51ff-a22c-69785c5b5b2f" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367678 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367678 1111 #uuid "a8405eef-0023-5f69-9a13-22ff7fd2e4e0" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367679 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367679 1111 #uuid "fbe918d3-f2d2-5b15-9537-25c15e291123" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367680 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367680 1111 #uuid "e9f3a5a8-4ed0-5fb8-85b2-0199c4929238" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367681 1034 "I only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367681 1111 #uuid "545b6df1-e7c9-5b96-be9b-3047d65b88b2" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367682 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367682 1111 #uuid "dbdba9cb-8d99-5e71-b72f-5aa8ee177eb4" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367683 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367684 13194139535812 true] [17592186367683 1089 "q-104995" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367683 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535812 true] [17592186367683 1100 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367683 1113 #uuid "6af8edba-09e4-5b04-83ba-ff9e01fbc281" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367683 1145 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367685 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367686 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367687 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367688 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367689 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367686 13194139535812 true] [17592186367684 1110 #uuid "edca972a-c43e-55a0-84fa-4d1fba93a955" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367685 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367685 1111 #uuid "8757c4ef-bd5c-5447-bc0b-2b9d8fd398ac" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367686 1034 "I only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367686 1111 #uuid "64b474ec-bb74-52e5-ac75-498b70c8f29a" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367687 1034 "III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367687 1111 #uuid "d7318c30-73d4-50b5-aa50-1c5747130ab9" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367688 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367688 1111 #uuid "cc3e04f5-42fc-5738-b049-fcbf23e9c6a4" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367689 1034 "II only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367689 1111 #uuid "88c387fe-9eaf-57ea-b9da-66b92be4c6c5" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367690 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367691 13194139535812 true] [17592186367690 1089 "q-105281" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367690 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535812 true] [17592186367690 1100 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367690 1113 #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367690 1145 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367692 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367693 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367694 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367695 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367696 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367692 13194139535812 true] [17592186367691 1110 #uuid "2c8d579d-8b0c-51aa-a4bb-9cc72e478714" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367692 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367692 1111 #uuid "a84def85-480b-5b20-aab4-9756748ec57b" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367693 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367693 1111 #uuid "1eae0aba-19a0-5c14-9cc2-e6715cbccb0c" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367694 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367694 1111 #uuid "035244ea-94da-5459-86da-9dc03ce65543" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367695 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367695 1111 #uuid "536b69a1-c752-5b37-b38a-f95603ac39fa" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367696 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ an