592186367704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535812 true] [17592186367704 1100 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139535812 true] [17592186367704 1113 #uuid "e1b5f6e8-a9c9-5be3-86f7-77f082bfcf04" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367704 1145 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2(0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=0\\cdot (-2)-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367706 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367707 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367708 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367709 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367710 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367707 13194139535812 true] [17592186367705 1110 #uuid "33795d29-fffc-526d-af18-f9bdc9afde14" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367706 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(e,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367706 1111 #uuid "bf464b6d-fd9f-5ab4-a32a-711279992