 minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367713 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367714 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367715 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367716 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367717 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367715 13194139535812 true] [17592186367712 1110 #uuid "3b4a61c8-b539-5756-8f15-590f50bf4ace" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367713 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367713 1111 #uuid "6d612087-0e03-5e0d-b954-0c8225f8fe86" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367714 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367714 1111 #uuid "c1ffd4d7-5dee-59ef-9b99-5c34684f7242" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367715 1034 "I only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367715 1111 #uuid "ac4388be-f3b7-53a0-b4aa-c26ef14e24dd" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367716 1034 "III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367716 1111 #uuid "7c98a72f-ab26-53d6-a5ed-fe1c17833a6f" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367717 1034 "II only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367717 1111 #uuid "a55bcecb-a983-5e1d-b3eb-df6f2b4d4e30" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367718 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367719 13194139535812 true] [17592186367718 1089 "q-80646" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367718 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535812 true] [17592186367718 1100 "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4xy+{y}^{2}.$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367718 1113 #uuid "37b606fb-2183-5f5c-9520-4ea005b108eb" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367718 1145 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =0 \\\\ -4x+(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(\\frac{1}{2})}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ We reject the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367720 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367721 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367722 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367723 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367724 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367722 13194139535812 true] [17592186367719 1110 #uuid "afe74ba8-78a7-570b-bd79-1be4d2c275d3" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367720 1034 "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367720 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367720 1111 #uuid "868b8a28-e0d2-5596-b16d-1936b3fb0767" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367721 1034 "(\\frac{1}{2},-1)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367721 1111 #uuid "214a1134-cd31-5d9f-bc61-e01bae2e7b0d" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367722 1034 "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367722 1111 #uuid "5e8ddad8-3775-5a1c-8240-4f0802adbff7" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367723 1034 "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367723 1111 #uuid "2987c67a-c6ff-53bf-9300-fbdda6968919" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367724 1034 "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535812 true] [17592186367724 1111 #uuid "c072f670-4486-5e92-ac0c-8f3170c8b1ac" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367725 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367726 13194139535812 true] [17592186367725 1089 "q-80685" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367725 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535812 true] [17592186367725 1100 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+6{y}^{2}.$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367725 1113 #uuid "61d61eae-7f7f-53de-97ab-4c73ee838831" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367725 1145 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367727 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367728 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367729 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367730 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367731 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367728 13194139535812 true] [17592186367726 1110 #uuid "6be81859-d6d8-5f72-9c2c-c34dcd30938a" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367727 1034 "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367727 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367727 1111 #uuid "5d85a239-eb69-5624-89e0-de2b205949b3" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367728 1034 "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367728 1111 #uuid "5af0e9ea-0b8f-514a-a763-9f121d875e20" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367729 1034 "$(0,1)$ and $(2,-1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367729 1111 #uuid "0059affa-2596-5d22-ba99-f5900753fcf7" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367730 1034 "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367730 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367730 1111 #uuid "7fe877e7-1722-5cae-8d3c-20f78a12752a" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367731 1034 "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367731 1111 #uuid "2ff96934-d06c-5531-a9b1-ee1ea4dd8f14" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367732 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367733 13194139535812 true] [17592186367732 1089 "q-81043" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535812 true] [17592186367732 1100 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139535812 true] [17592186367732 1113 #uuid "d6d8bf42-1a66-5e36-832b-b1d8f827ac5e" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367732 1145 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1 & 2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1=0 \\\\ 2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-1)={e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=1\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=2>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367734 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367735 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367736 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367737 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367738 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367737 13194139535812 true] [17592186367733 1110 #uuid "5e8c87ff-6cc8-534f-bce4-4dd6bc118911" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367734 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367734 1111 #uuid "4f754cf3-a0b6-51f8-8e46-bc8c19c0b418" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367735 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367735 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367735 1111 #uuid "5da179a1-fb2a-5322-ba79-f4a4519548a2" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367736 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367736 1111 #uuid "44d840cc-1be2-5890-9dd2-7dca3a7f5c39" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367737 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367737 1111 #uuid "b4599e1c-98c3-5d3a-b58b-cf1d8bab3f16" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367738 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367738 1111 #uuid "76bedb92-371c-545b-be54-437de68cbc21" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367740 13194139535812 true] [17592186367739 1089 "q-81164" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535812 true] [17592186367739 1100 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367739 1113 #uuid "6879580a-ed3c-5479-82f5-d8d6e4ea2d37" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367739 1145 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367741 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367742 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367743 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367744 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367745 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367745 13194139535812 true] [17592186367740 1110 #uuid "2a8ef7df-4c8f-5a39-85be-bf895e8f3f4f" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367741 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367741 1111 #uuid "7b40a2ea-486c-5467-9387-5a9c64c59ce9" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367742 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367742 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367742 1111 #uuid "602adfd2-4490-59e5-a8fb-a759398eac50" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367743 1034 "I only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367743 1111 #uuid "cf5656a4-a613-5331-8fe8-c67dfdf7bdd5" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367744 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367744 1111 #uuid "b7ad5206-6c7d-5ccf-9fbe-9196224992b4" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367745 1034 "II only" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367745 1111 #uuid "4a3a6421-f2d3-58d7-9cc2-a1286cb3e99a" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367746 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367747 13194139535812 true] [17592186367746 1089 "q-81167" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367746 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535812 true] [17592186367746 1100 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139535812 true] [17592186367746 1113 #uuid "e1be0ef8-da54-5ba5-b724-bceca43b6b0a" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367746 1145 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367748 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367749 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367750 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367751 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367752 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1041 "selection" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367751 13194139535812 true] [17592186367747 1110 #uuid "38ce8d3a-1d12-54db-af78-d4b96622d008" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367748 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367748 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367748 1111 #uuid "fe283b24-dc9f-5f7f-b7f6-e2ba52d2b996" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367749 1034 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$" 13194139535812 true] [17592186367749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535812 true] [17592186367749 1111 #uuid "0c8539ec-ed32-52